1
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:12,299
This is a 1976 Ford Country Squire.

2
00:00:13,766 --> 00:00:14,966
I don't like this car.

3
00:00:15,366 --> 00:00:17,233
It's big. It's wasteful.

4
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,300
It's got stupid, fake wood
paneling on the side.

5
00:00:20,433 --> 00:00:23,500
It's an L-T-D. L-T-D stands for "Limited."

6
00:00:23,566 --> 00:00:25,000
You know what it's limited to?

7
00:00:25,066 --> 00:00:28,566
How many of these damn things we can sell,
that's what it's limited to.

8
00:00:30,333 --> 00:00:33,433
I'm hating every second of this,
but I'm doing it.

9
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:36,366
Because this car belongs
to a very special friend of mine.

10
00:00:36,733 --> 00:00:38,633
And that's the only reason
it's on the show.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,633
And she just bought this.
I don't even know why.

12
00:00:41,699 --> 00:00:43,800
But I'm gonna find out today.

13
00:00:43,866 --> 00:00:46,466
On a brand-new episode
of Comedians in Cars...

14
00:00:47,599 --> 00:00:49,300
Getting aggravated.

15
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:54,166
You called for a comedian coffee service?

16
00:00:54,766 --> 00:00:56,800
Constantly! They finally picked up.

17
00:00:59,766 --> 00:01:03,099
[Sarah] I can't get over
how gorgeous this is.

18
00:01:03,163 --> 00:01:04,397
[Jerry] Why is it gorgeous?

19
00:01:04,464 --> 00:01:06,099
There's just a huge amount of glass.

20
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:08,334
You know, I grew up in a car like this.

21
00:01:08,435 --> 00:01:10,470
I spent so much time
in the back of that car.

22
00:01:10,537 --> 00:01:12,872
-God, smell it. A little bit of gas.
-[Jerry laughs]

23
00:01:12,966 --> 00:01:15,566
Do you know when you smell in these
big old American cars?

24
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:17,633
-Yeah.
-A teeny bit of gas... which I love.

25
00:01:17,966 --> 00:01:21,100
There's just so much space.
My kids are gonna freak out.

26
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:23,133
Well, of course there's
a lot of room. It's huge.

27
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,033
And there's still some storage space.

28
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:27,000
Yeah, so the packages can hit you

29
00:01:27,066 --> 00:01:28,600
-as the car takes off.
-Exactly.

30
00:01:28,666 --> 00:01:30,600
-Well, you probably deserve it.
-Yeah.

31
00:01:30,666 --> 00:01:32,833
[both laugh]

32
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,733
That's a nice seat. Come on!
Did you feel it?

33
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:41,566
I honestly cannot believe
we are in this car and that it's mine.

34
00:01:41,666 --> 00:01:44,166
Hey, what's up?
What do you think of this car?

35
00:01:44,233 --> 00:01:45,066
[Jerry singing]

36
00:01:45,133 --> 00:01:48,000
♪ I think it's time for that silly show ♪ 

37
00:01:48,508 --> 00:01:50,643
♪ Where comedians in their cars go ♪

38
00:01:50,733 --> 00:01:53,766
♪ They talk and laugh with coffee beans ♪ 

39
00:01:53,866 --> 00:01:56,133
♪ And drink and stir 
And wear blue jeans ♪

40
00:01:56,199 --> 00:01:59,666
♪ So I'll get in that car ♪ 

41
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:01,866
♪ No need to go far ♪ 

42
00:02:01,966 --> 00:02:05,233
♪ Sometimes on stage I won't come offie ♪ 

43
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:08,533
♪ Unless I get in a car for coffee ♪ 

44
00:02:14,433 --> 00:02:15,799
Turn the... Yeah, I wanna hear it.

45
00:02:16,066 --> 00:02:17,933
[turn signal sound]

46
00:02:18,066 --> 00:02:21,700
When I was little,
that sound was like the score of my life.

47
00:02:22,166 --> 00:02:25,266
You know, it was so loud.
Do you remember how loud turn signals...

48
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:26,966
They don't even make noise now.

49
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:29,366
Any car insanity, I love.

50
00:02:32,433 --> 00:02:34,966
[Sarah] I have a lot of good memories
in a car like this.

51
00:02:35,033 --> 00:02:38,066
And I have sentimental memories.
I'm wistful about some of it.

52
00:02:38,133 --> 00:02:40,900
Some really great times
in my formative years.

53
00:02:42,666 --> 00:02:45,633
[Jerry] I have to say,
I kind of like driving this thing.

54
00:02:45,765 --> 00:02:48,067
Did you feel how it went
over the bumps? How nice it was?

55
00:02:48,134 --> 00:02:50,236
Of course. I feel everything,
see everything, know everything.

56
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:51,166
I know.

57
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:53,433
[Jerry] You and I are very funny together.

58
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,066
We're both a little hyper-aware.

59
00:02:56,133 --> 00:02:58,433
-Of little things.
-Of little things and...

60
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:00,600
-Bless you. Uh-oh.
-Excuse me.

61
00:03:00,666 --> 00:03:02,933
You know, in the old days,
they'd have a box of Kleenex.

62
00:03:03,466 --> 00:03:05,600
Or your mom would pull one out
or something.

63
00:03:08,588 --> 00:03:10,957
[Jerry] Don't you love New York
going to work in the morning?

64
00:03:11,033 --> 00:03:12,466
[Sarah] Love it.

65
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:15,566
Look at her.
Look at the lady. Looking good.

66
00:03:17,764 --> 00:03:19,432
[Jerry]
How you enjoying the ride so far?

67
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:20,333
[Sarah] I love it.

68
00:03:20,399 --> 00:03:23,799
I got so much glass. I can see.
There's almost no obstruction...

69
00:03:23,866 --> 00:03:25,733
with all of 14th Street.

70
00:03:25,799 --> 00:03:27,533
-Was your thought...
-Top.

71
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:28,533
Family vacation.

72
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:30,533
-All the luggage is up there.
-Right.

73
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:33,433
Kids on an Etch A Sketch in the back.
Mad Libs.

74
00:03:33,479 --> 00:03:36,649
Water colors, and you don't have it.
You just wet the brush yourself.

75
00:03:40,966 --> 00:03:42,666
-[Sarah] We going to the tunnel?
-[Jerry] Yeah.

76
00:03:42,733 --> 00:03:43,766
What I would have done,

77
00:03:43,899 --> 00:03:46,000
I would have made a left on Bleecker,
left on 10th,

78
00:03:46,066 --> 00:03:49,000
and I would have taken 10th Street
straight over till 3rd,

79
00:03:49,066 --> 00:03:51,299
and then come around
to the Midtown Tunnel that way.

80
00:03:51,366 --> 00:03:52,966
-That's what we're doing.
-Okay.

81
00:03:53,033 --> 00:03:55,566
-So next time we do this...
-Yeah.

82
00:03:55,633 --> 00:03:58,733
...in this car, in that outfit,
we'll do it my way.

83
00:03:58,799 --> 00:04:00,333
I'm kidding.

84
00:04:03,466 --> 00:04:05,566
-[Jerry] I'm so hungry.
-[Sarah] I'm so sorry.

85
00:04:05,766 --> 00:04:08,299
-I really just want a coffee.
-Well, do you want me to get...

86
00:04:08,366 --> 00:04:10,766
I'm happy to run and get...
There's coffee right there.

87
00:04:10,966 --> 00:04:14,033
[Jerry] Look at this lady. Hello.
How big an object does it take?

88
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:16,300
[Sarah] Gotta talk about that.
How do you like your coffee?

89
00:04:16,366 --> 00:04:18,666
-Let me go with you.
-No, stay. You gotta watch this car.

90
00:04:18,725 --> 00:04:21,227
If something happens to this car,
I will never forgive you.

91
00:04:23,963 --> 00:04:26,399
-[Jerry] What do you think?
-[man] Grocery-getter. I like it.

92
00:04:26,466 --> 00:04:28,333
[Jerry]
You can get a grocery with anything.

93
00:04:29,435 --> 00:04:30,436
Who's with the car?

94
00:04:30,503 --> 00:04:31,371
-Hi.
-Hi.

95
00:04:31,571 --> 00:04:33,439
Could we get a small coffee
for this gentleman?

96
00:04:37,833 --> 00:04:39,733
[Sarah] Hard to not stare
at that car, right?

97
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:41,966
[man] My father used to have
a whole bunch of these.

98
00:04:42,033 --> 00:04:44,433
-They're worth something now.
-They are.

99
00:04:48,433 --> 00:04:50,833
-[Sarah] Want me to hold that for you?
-Sure, thanks.

100
00:04:50,966 --> 00:04:53,300
-The old-school cup holder, people.
-Yeah.

101
00:04:53,433 --> 00:04:56,000
[Jerry] I feel the cup holder defines
our culture.

102
00:04:56,099 --> 00:04:57,700
I'm not gripping anything.

103
00:04:59,366 --> 00:05:03,166
People get in, "Where is the cup holder
in this car? This is insane."

104
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:06,966
And I first discovered it with Amazon.com.

105
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:08,400
They have the one-click ordering.

106
00:05:08,466 --> 00:05:09,766
-Yeah.
-One click, that's it.

107
00:05:09,833 --> 00:05:12,233
Need me to click twice?
I don't even want it anymore.

108
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:14,033
You think I got nothing to do but click?

109
00:05:15,099 --> 00:05:18,233
You make it one click or I'm out of here!

110
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,800
[Sarah] So interesting,
because I think about the television set.

111
00:05:22,866 --> 00:05:25,333
There was a period of time
in which we had to stand up

112
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:28,433
and go switch it, then sit down.

113
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:31,933
See what your experience was like,
sitting down, watching that show.

114
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:33,933
-Well, let's see how this is.
-Yeah.

115
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:38,900
[Sarah] But there are things that
I am sorry don't exist for my kids now.

116
00:05:38,966 --> 00:05:42,033
The way we kind of communicated
with each other.

117
00:05:42,099 --> 00:05:43,566
Or a busy signal.

118
00:05:43,633 --> 00:05:46,500
The anticipation that a busy signal
can build up and up.

119
00:05:46,766 --> 00:05:47,800
Yeah, yeah.

120
00:05:47,866 --> 00:05:50,900
Someone coming to our house
because the phone was busy for so long.

121
00:05:50,966 --> 00:05:52,633
She was in a rage,
one of my mother's friends.

122
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:54,733
Like, "Is Barbara there?"
"Well, she's on..."

123
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:57,400
"I know she's on the phone.
I've been calling for 45 minutes."

124
00:05:57,566 --> 00:05:59,900
[Sarah] There are certain things
about that that I do miss.

125
00:05:59,966 --> 00:06:01,400
[Jerry] I miss every second of it.

126
00:06:01,466 --> 00:06:07,233
But I can't escape what, I feel,
must be some narcissism.

127
00:06:07,566 --> 00:06:09,566
I grew up in the suburbs. Didn't like it.

128
00:06:10,033 --> 00:06:11,733
-Always wanted to live in the city.
-Yeah.

129
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:13,833
Now I wanna live in the suburbs.

130
00:06:14,733 --> 00:06:17,766
You want your kids to want out
the way you wanted out.

131
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,200
No, I just want them to live my life over.

132
00:06:21,266 --> 00:06:23,233
-Yeah.
-That's narcissism.

133
00:06:26,833 --> 00:06:30,900
[Jerry] Matthew has this great line
about when you get your kid a spoon,

134
00:06:30,966 --> 00:06:33,433
you think, "What a great dad I am.

135
00:06:34,066 --> 00:06:36,466
Look at me getting my kid a spoon."

136
00:06:36,966 --> 00:06:38,766
-That is what we think.
-Yeah.

137
00:06:38,866 --> 00:06:40,933
Why are we such horrible parents?

138
00:06:41,033 --> 00:06:43,933
-Oh, my God. Are we horrible parents?
-I think so.

139
00:06:45,366 --> 00:06:47,633
See, look at that.
Why are you so upset about it?

140
00:06:47,799 --> 00:06:50,633
You've already run rings around
what your mother has done.

141
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,633
And that's not good enough for your kid.

142
00:06:52,799 --> 00:06:54,966
[Jerry] This is probably
a longer conversation

143
00:06:55,033 --> 00:06:57,166
about parenting that we've just had

144
00:06:57,233 --> 00:07:00,466
than your mother had all told her life.

145
00:07:00,533 --> 00:07:01,633
Because she never had time.

146
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:05,000
-And who cared?
-Plus, she was always in a rage.

147
00:07:07,433 --> 00:07:08,599
Legitimately.

148
00:07:08,666 --> 00:07:09,666
"I'm gonna slam you!"

149
00:07:09,733 --> 00:07:12,299
I remember my mother saying,
"I'll turn around and slam you."

150
00:07:12,366 --> 00:07:13,733
That's what she used to say.

151
00:07:13,799 --> 00:07:16,066
My friend, Mark Schiff, says,
"Mothers don't have headaches,

152
00:07:16,133 --> 00:07:17,700
they always have splitting headaches."

153
00:07:19,299 --> 00:07:22,400
You don't think it could be
from screaming nine hours a day, do you?

154
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:32,666
[Sarah] Did you ever care
about a motorcycle?

155
00:07:32,733 --> 00:07:34,033
[Jerry] I had many motorcycles.

156
00:07:34,766 --> 00:07:36,933
And I obsessed in my young life,

157
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,333
"I'm either gonna be a motorcycle guy
or I'm gonna be a sports car guy.

158
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:43,533
I gotta decide who I am and what I am.

159
00:07:43,599 --> 00:07:46,400
And that will be determined
by how I get around."

160
00:07:46,466 --> 00:07:49,400
And there was a guy who worked
in my dad's shop. He was a motorcycle guy.

161
00:07:49,466 --> 00:07:51,633
And he came in one day,
and it was pouring rain,

162
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:53,299
and he was soaking wet.

163
00:07:53,366 --> 00:07:56,599
And I looked at him.
Here, I'm like 17 years old and I go,

164
00:07:56,666 --> 00:07:59,833
"So, today, don't you wish you had a car?"

165
00:08:00,799 --> 00:08:03,333
And he says,
"Nope. Just wish it wasn't raining."

166
00:08:03,533 --> 00:08:06,200
-Wow.
-[jazz music playing]

167
00:08:07,466 --> 00:08:10,533
[Jerry] Do you remember
Then Came Bronson with Michael Parks?

168
00:08:10,599 --> 00:08:13,933
It was a great antihero
TV show of the '70s.

169
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:15,766
I'm gonna do the opening,
word for word.

170
00:08:15,828 --> 00:08:16,696
Yeah, okay.

171
00:08:20,799 --> 00:08:23,033
[Jerry] Michael Parks 
was like TV Steve McQueen.

172
00:08:23,099 --> 00:08:25,400
He pulls up to a light.
This is the opening of the show.

173
00:08:25,466 --> 00:08:28,066
A guy in this car pulls up next to him.

174
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:30,566
He's got the Don Draper little hat on.

175
00:08:30,633 --> 00:08:34,033
He's got the suit, he's got a
wife and kids and a job, and he's trapped.

176
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:35,333
He says, "Where you going?"

177
00:08:35,633 --> 00:08:38,366
-Where to?
-Says, "Oh, I don't know.

178
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:40,733
Wherever I end up, I guess."

179
00:08:40,799 --> 00:08:42,833
I don't know. Wherever I end up, I guess.

180
00:08:43,389 --> 00:08:44,323
Wow.

181
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:47,133
Husband guy says, "Sure wish I was you."

182
00:08:47,433 --> 00:08:48,900
Well, I wish I was you.

183
00:08:49,066 --> 00:08:52,733
And Michael Parks goes,
"Yeah, well, hang in there."

184
00:08:54,033 --> 00:08:54,934
Well, hang in there.

185
00:08:57,533 --> 00:08:58,833
And he roars off.

186
00:09:01,574 --> 00:09:03,242
Coolest thing I ever saw in my life.

187
00:09:06,699 --> 00:09:09,733
[Sarah] You know, there used
to be a sign on buildings in New York

188
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,833
that basically said,
"No Irish, no show people..."

189
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:14,966
-What?
-"No..." Yeah.

190
00:09:15,066 --> 00:09:16,766
-A "no Irish" sign?
-Not...

191
00:09:16,833 --> 00:09:19,433
I might be embroidering
the story a little bit.

192
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:21,733
But basically, it was like,
"Show folks need not apply."

193
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:23,266
-Show folks?
-Right.

194
00:09:23,333 --> 00:09:26,066
Irish. And show folks.

195
00:09:26,132 --> 00:09:29,135
Well, I think there was
a certain sort of class of people

196
00:09:29,233 --> 00:09:32,133
-that was thought of as beneath...
-Irish show folks.

197
00:09:32,199 --> 00:09:35,266
-And God forbid you were Irish.
-The lowest.

198
00:09:35,333 --> 00:09:36,466
Show folk.

199
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,733
We can't do that anymore.
That's not politic.

200
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:43,500
I believe Irish show folk
are allowed everywhere now.

201
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:46,833
[Sarah] So, here's what I was thinking of.

202
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:50,466
I might do a patty melt on rye bread.

203
00:09:50,533 --> 00:09:53,666
I've never heard someone accent
the "bread" in "rye bread."

204
00:09:54,133 --> 00:09:56,733
-I didn't mean to. Rye bread.
-Rye bread.

205
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:59,333
[Jerry] "I'll have some rye bread,"
I've heard that.

206
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:02,166
-"I'll have some rye bread."
-Rye bread.

207
00:10:02,233 --> 00:10:03,466
[Jerry] I have not.

208
00:10:04,266 --> 00:10:06,833
[Sarah] How did he get
the word out? How did he...

209
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:08,699
[Jerry] Good question.
Here's how he did it.

210
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:09,933
[ice cream truck music]

211
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,133
That's the ice cream man. I'm not kidding.

212
00:10:12,199 --> 00:10:13,633
-Do you hear it? There he is.
-Yes.

213
00:10:14,613 --> 00:10:17,416
[Sarah] Ah, that's a sound
that coincides with this car.

214
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:18,833
But here's the difference.

215
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:21,833
Just the other neighborhood kids had it,
not us. Not the Parker kids.

216
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:24,333
-[Jerry] No ice cream?
-[Sarah] A half-gallon of Breyers...

217
00:10:24,433 --> 00:10:26,300
maybe once every two to three months.

218
00:10:26,466 --> 00:10:28,699
And it was gone in about 24 hours.

219
00:10:28,766 --> 00:10:31,466
And my mom was like,
"Who ate all the ice cream?"

220
00:10:36,335 --> 00:10:37,903
[Jerry] Can't tell you
how happy it makes me

221
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,466
just driving on these streets
where I grew up.

222
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:45,433
So, imagine what a car or a bike meant
when you lived in a place like this.

223
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:47,333
It's like angel wings.

224
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,699
What role did your mother play
in your life?

225
00:10:52,766 --> 00:10:55,433
Was there an exchange of,
"How was your day?"

226
00:10:55,533 --> 00:10:57,300
-No.
-Was there music on?

227
00:10:57,366 --> 00:10:59,366
-[Jerry] Cha-cha.
-[Sarah] And what about the day?

228
00:10:59,433 --> 00:11:01,833
How would she keep
her mind occupied? No TV?

229
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:04,100
-No.
-No radio?

230
00:11:04,163 --> 00:11:05,431
-No.
-Wow.

231
00:11:05,900 --> 00:11:07,666
[Sarah] Can you imagine sitting
in a quiet house?

232
00:11:08,033 --> 00:11:10,633
-But did they hug you? Hold you?
-No.

233
00:11:10,699 --> 00:11:12,233
-Kiss you a lot?
-No.

234
00:11:12,300 --> 00:11:13,666
Did you see a smile on her face?

235
00:11:13,766 --> 00:11:16,133
No one in my life has ever asked me
any of these questions.

236
00:11:16,199 --> 00:11:17,666
Well, I'm really curious.

237
00:11:17,766 --> 00:11:20,300
And look how interesting...
That house looks actually old.

238
00:11:24,383 --> 00:11:25,751
[no audible dialogue]

239
00:11:36,228 --> 00:11:38,964
Oh, classic chicken salad, Caesar.

240
00:11:39,033 --> 00:11:40,933
-Do you have a preference?
-L'Oréal.

241
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:42,433
[laughs]

242
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:43,900
Hi, how are you guys?

243
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:45,266
-How are you?
-Good. How you doing?

244
00:11:45,304 --> 00:11:46,639
[waitress] Something to drink?

245
00:11:46,706 --> 00:11:49,108
-Chocolate egg cream.
-And a cup of coffee, please.

246
00:11:49,199 --> 00:11:51,000
-And you can drink coffee all day?
-All day.

247
00:11:51,233 --> 00:11:54,033
-It would give me horrible anxiety.
-I like the anxiety.

248
00:12:01,300 --> 00:12:03,833
So, I'll have the patty melt on rye bread.

249
00:12:04,023 --> 00:12:06,225
-Do you have rye bread?
-[indistinct]

250
00:12:06,358 --> 00:12:07,193
Rye bread.

251
00:12:08,933 --> 00:12:10,366
[Jerry] I do this bit with the kids.

252
00:12:10,433 --> 00:12:12,400
It's called
"the guy who doesn't know where to look."

253
00:12:12,866 --> 00:12:13,933
What's that?

254
00:12:14,233 --> 00:12:16,836
Know when people tell a story
that goes, "So I get on the train."

255
00:12:17,133 --> 00:12:18,133
Yeah.

256
00:12:18,199 --> 00:12:21,900
And the guy,
"No, it's not actually there."

257
00:12:22,533 --> 00:12:24,033
"So I'm walking down the street..."

258
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:25,266
[laughs]

259
00:12:25,533 --> 00:12:27,300
And you look where the guy...

260
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:29,366
-That's so funny.
-You can have it.

261
00:12:29,433 --> 00:12:30,633
You can do it in your family.

262
00:12:30,699 --> 00:12:31,800
-Can't do it.
-Why?

263
00:12:31,866 --> 00:12:34,233
Some things might be wasted
on four-and-a-half-year-olds.

264
00:12:34,300 --> 00:12:35,566
Nothing is wasted.

265
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:42,733
-Holy cow!
-Yeah.

266
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:44,933
I've never seen it like that. Open-faced.

267
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,633
[waitress] Do you want Parmesan cheese?

268
00:12:46,699 --> 00:12:48,066
[Jerry] It could use some cheese.

269
00:12:49,933 --> 00:12:53,766
What do you think people think we're doing
that are watching us right now?

270
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:55,933
A new TV show, I guarantee you.

271
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,266
I know, but this doesn't look like a show.

272
00:12:58,333 --> 00:13:01,266
Why would reason have anything
to do with what people think?

273
00:13:01,333 --> 00:13:03,166
That's your best line of the day so far.

274
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:06,766
[Sarah] And I was thinking...

275
00:13:06,833 --> 00:13:09,233
So, all these people are gonna go home

276
00:13:09,300 --> 00:13:11,633
and they're gonna go to sleep
in their apartments.

277
00:13:11,733 --> 00:13:13,366
Like, everybody sleeps.

278
00:13:13,633 --> 00:13:15,766
Everybody's gonna lie down.

279
00:13:16,066 --> 00:13:18,566
Think about it. All over the city,

280
00:13:18,633 --> 00:13:20,933
there's just people sleeping.

281
00:13:21,099 --> 00:13:23,566
It's so weird.

282
00:13:23,633 --> 00:13:26,766
-You sound like you're high.
-And I'm not though, I know.

283
00:13:26,833 --> 00:13:28,833
You really remind me of me.

284
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,666
-Really?
-I see why I exhaust people.

285
00:13:32,378 --> 00:13:34,947
[laughs]

286
00:13:35,033 --> 00:13:36,099
That's horrible.

287
00:13:38,733 --> 00:13:42,166
Okay. Now, you told us
the perfect morning with an espresso.

288
00:13:42,233 --> 00:13:45,133
First of all, to wake and be rested.
Number one, let's just start with that.

289
00:13:45,433 --> 00:13:47,800
Take the kids to school,
which I genuinely enjoy.

290
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:49,266
It's Fall or Spring.

291
00:13:49,333 --> 00:13:51,699
The kids are much easier
to get out the door.

292
00:13:51,766 --> 00:13:53,033
They're much nicer about it.

293
00:13:53,099 --> 00:13:56,933
You're not in as many battles about layers

294
00:13:57,266 --> 00:13:58,433
and the government,

295
00:13:58,566 --> 00:14:00,333
which I always bring
into the conversation.

296
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:01,733
-Why?
-Like, "The government says

297
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:02,800
you have to wear socks."

298
00:14:02,866 --> 00:14:04,533
"Don't make me call the government."

299
00:14:04,866 --> 00:14:06,633
That's a great parenting tip.

300
00:14:06,699 --> 00:14:07,666
Yeah, the government.

301
00:14:10,066 --> 00:14:11,233
Can I get you anything else?

302
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:13,699
-No. I couldn't imagine...
-No coffee?

303
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:18,000
-Wowza.
-Yes.

304
00:14:21,433 --> 00:14:23,966
Here's a check take me and Larry Miller
used to do all the time.

305
00:14:24,033 --> 00:14:26,933
"So, I thought maybe later
we could grab..."

306
00:14:29,300 --> 00:14:30,533
You just keep talking,

307
00:14:30,599 --> 00:14:33,366
-but your eyes can't help but react.
-Yeah.

308
00:14:33,433 --> 00:14:36,199
So, I thought maybe
if you drove the car back that we could...

309
00:14:38,866 --> 00:14:41,566
Are you waiting for me
to offer up some cash?

310
00:14:41,633 --> 00:14:43,166
Should I feign...

311
00:14:43,533 --> 00:14:45,766
How many more times
are you gonna laugh at that?

312
00:14:46,033 --> 00:14:47,166
I don't know.

313
00:14:47,733 --> 00:14:49,466
Okay, the check is $37.

314
00:14:49,533 --> 00:14:51,633
-How much tip would you leave?
-Ten.

315
00:14:52,157 --> 00:14:53,325
[sputters]

316
00:14:53,966 --> 00:14:56,266
-You go to hell.
-That's not enough?

317
00:14:56,333 --> 00:14:59,166
-No.
-Wait. You move the decimals.

318
00:14:59,233 --> 00:15:02,400
It was a $40 check.
You would leave $8, would be 20%.

319
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:05,733
No. Am I... Somebody help me.

320
00:15:06,033 --> 00:15:07,433
I'll help you.

321
00:15:08,333 --> 00:15:09,733
Because everyone's gonna ask her.

322
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:12,666
"Oh, my God, you waited on Jerry Seinfeld
and Sarah Jessica Parker.

323
00:15:12,733 --> 00:15:14,333
-How much did they tip you?"
-How much did...

324
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:15,733
What do you want her to say?

325
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:18,000
-Twenty bucks.
-Yeah. Is that what you want?

326
00:15:18,066 --> 00:15:20,800
-Ten. You just move the decimal point.
-Okay, wait.

327
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:22,766
You're going to leave...

328
00:15:22,821 --> 00:15:25,491
Wow. Big shot. Unbelievable.

329
00:15:25,566 --> 00:15:27,133
-Gross?
-Disgusting.

330
00:15:27,199 --> 00:15:29,199
-Garish?
-Vulgar.

331
00:15:30,333 --> 00:15:33,166
No, it's lovely. I'm a total... [bleep]

332
00:15:33,233 --> 00:15:36,666
-But for real, like, all joking aside...
-What is real?

333
00:15:37,199 --> 00:15:39,800
You tell me what real is, and we'll
have gotten something outta this.

334
00:15:39,866 --> 00:15:41,366
I'm really glad we are not married.

335
00:15:41,533 --> 00:15:43,066
[laughs]

336
00:15:51,199 --> 00:15:52,866
-[Jerry] Sarah, you drive.
-[Sarah] You serious?

337
00:15:52,933 --> 00:15:54,966
-Don't you want to?
-Yes, of course I do.

338
00:15:55,166 --> 00:15:57,400
[Sarah] But the seat's too far back.
Can we move the seat?

339
00:15:57,466 --> 00:15:59,699
-Sure, there's a little...
-I remember my mom in the old days...

340
00:15:59,766 --> 00:16:02,099
Yeah, the whole thing, you have to...

341
00:16:02,166 --> 00:16:05,533
The entire family has to get
behind the moving of the seat.

342
00:16:05,699 --> 00:16:07,033
On three.

343
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:08,800
[laughs]

344
00:16:11,300 --> 00:16:13,733
-Oh, yeah.
-Look at you.

345
00:16:13,900 --> 00:16:16,099
Look at the confidence. I love it.

346
00:16:16,166 --> 00:16:18,133
You are really a mom now.

347
00:16:18,199 --> 00:16:21,866
I feel like I'm a kid getting dropped off
to go bowling or a movie.

348
00:16:21,933 --> 00:16:23,199
[laughs]

349
00:16:24,466 --> 00:16:27,733
-Going north?
-Yeah, the black SUV with the...

350
00:16:27,933 --> 00:16:29,366
-With the camera sticking out of it?
-Yes.

351
00:16:29,433 --> 00:16:30,533
One of my other favorite takes.

352
00:16:30,599 --> 00:16:32,500
This is similar
to the check take, the eye roll.

353
00:16:32,566 --> 00:16:34,966
You go, "The one with the camera?"
And I go, "Yes."

354
00:16:42,333 --> 00:16:43,933
-Oh, dear.
-Oh, dearie.

355
00:16:44,566 --> 00:16:46,866
Merge, merging. Oh, dear.

356
00:16:46,966 --> 00:16:48,400
[Jerry] That way, that way, that way.

357
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:49,742
-[Sarah] Can I do it?
-Yeah.

358
00:16:49,808 --> 00:16:50,876
-[horn honking]
-Yeah.

359
00:16:51,176 --> 00:16:54,179
[Sarah] Hey, come on, what's up?
It's 1976. Get over it.

360
00:16:55,599 --> 00:16:57,966
[Jerry] Oh, this seat belt is so brutal!

361
00:16:59,400 --> 00:17:02,166
God, it just clicks and clicks.

362
00:17:02,221 --> 00:17:04,223
-[horn honking]
-[Sarah shrieks]

363
00:17:04,466 --> 00:17:08,099
Sorry. You're not gonna trust me,
I'm actually a really good driver.

364
00:17:08,866 --> 00:17:10,966
-How's that seat belt?
-It's horrible.

365
00:17:11,733 --> 00:17:14,899
I gotta hold it so it's not like
getting lap-band surgery.

366
00:17:15,399 --> 00:17:17,200
I hope that... Sorry.

367
00:17:18,099 --> 00:17:20,333
[laughs]

368
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:23,200
Oh, my God.

369
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:30,966
Why do they always have this on radios?

370
00:17:31,033 --> 00:17:33,000
-The five and the four.
-I don't know.

371
00:17:33,066 --> 00:17:35,066
-They've always had that on AM.
-You're right.

372
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:37,000
-Why the four?
-What is that?

373
00:17:37,066 --> 00:17:39,600
-What is the four?
-I don't know.

374
00:17:39,666 --> 00:17:42,133
Oh, my God. That's a great observation.

375
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:45,166
[Sarah] If this were my mom,
she would have had

376
00:17:45,233 --> 00:17:47,666
Carole King Tapestry
playing on the 8-track.

377
00:17:47,733 --> 00:17:51,066
The song about, "Hey, Dad,
can I borrow your car keys?" Harry Chapin.

378
00:17:51,133 --> 00:17:54,866
That song, "Cat's Cradle,"
messed up a lot of dads.

379
00:17:54,933 --> 00:17:57,300
And Harry Chapin died on this highway.

380
00:17:57,366 --> 00:18:02,533
He had a massive coronary while driving
a VW Beetle on the Long Island Expressway.

381
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:05,133
And I recorded a song with him
right before he died.

382
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:06,600
-Get out of here.
-Yeah.

383
00:18:06,666 --> 00:18:09,333
-You recorded with Harry Chapin?
-Me and my brothers and sisters.

384
00:18:09,466 --> 00:18:11,933
♪ There are so many colors 
In the rainbow ♪ 

385
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,166
♪ So many colors in the morning sun ♪ 

386
00:18:14,233 --> 00:18:17,166
♪ So many colors in a flower
And I see every one ♪

387
00:18:22,868 --> 00:18:26,405
♪ There are so many colors 
In the rainbow ♪

388
00:18:26,472 --> 00:18:29,308
♪ So many colors in the morning sun ♪

389
00:18:29,374 --> 00:18:34,980
♪ So many colors in a flower
And I see every one ♪

390
00:18:35,047 --> 00:18:37,883
♪ So many colors in a flower ♪

391
00:18:37,950 --> 00:18:40,719
♪ And I see every one ♪

392
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:48,500
[Sarah] I hear James Wilkie talk
about his future,

393
00:18:48,566 --> 00:18:51,266
and it just makes me wonder
what you thought about yours.

394
00:18:51,333 --> 00:18:53,566
What you wanted to do,
what you loved to do.

395
00:18:53,633 --> 00:18:55,866
Okay, here's what I thought
when I was his age.

396
00:18:56,166 --> 00:19:01,133
"The world is great
because it has bicycles.

397
00:19:02,033 --> 00:19:04,500
It has toys.

398
00:19:04,566 --> 00:19:06,600
And it has cute girls."

399
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:09,566
And then I saw
my first Tampax commercial...

400
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:13,833
And I thought,
"There may be more to that thing..."

401
00:19:13,899 --> 00:19:15,500
[laughs]

402
00:19:17,066 --> 00:19:18,733
"...than meets the eye."

403
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:20,800
I knew the first two are solid.

404
00:19:21,066 --> 00:19:23,100
-Toys, bicycles.
-Bicycles, right.

405
00:19:23,166 --> 00:19:24,733
You didn't see
any weird bicycle commercials.

406
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:27,166
No. These are the things
that made the world great for me.

407
00:19:27,233 --> 00:19:28,300
Yeah, yeah.

408
00:19:28,366 --> 00:19:30,366
The world's gonna be great.
I'm gonna love this place.

409
00:19:30,433 --> 00:19:32,100
I'm gonna love being a human.

410
00:19:33,833 --> 00:19:36,300
Toys, bikes, cute girls.

411
00:19:39,966 --> 00:19:42,866
There might be more to this one. Yeah.

